<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jezebel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omride-dynasty">Omride dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jezebel was a Phoenician princess, the daughter of Ethbaal, king of the Sidonians, who married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab of Israel</a> in the ninth century BCE. The Books of Kings portray her as a promoter of the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> and an enemy of the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a>, and tell how she was thrown from a window at Jezreel on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>'s orders after his coup. Her name became a byword for a wicked woman.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jezebel.jpg" alt="Jezebel" width="320" height="587" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Byam Liston Shaw · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Liston_Byam_Shaw_003.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Close ties with the Phoenician cities, sealed by Ahab’s marriage to <b>Jezebel</b>, daughter of the king of Sidon, brought luxury goods and craftsmen, and the carved ivory panels found at Samaria show the court’s taste for Phoenician work.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hebrew Bible, which calls Sidon the mother city of Tyre and says the Sidonians long oppressed Israel, records Solomon's marriage alliance with the Sidonians and names <b>Jezebel</b> a daughter of King Ithobaal I of Sidon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 9th century BCE Ithobaal I of Tyre, whom the Books of Kings call Ethbaal, king of the Sidonians, married his daughter <b>Jezebel</b> to Ahab, king of Israel, an alliance the biblical writers remembered with hostility.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Society</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. Died in Tel Jezreel, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.56,35.38&amp;year=-835" aria-label="Sidon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5453 946 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jezebel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5903 1216h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5903 1216h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.56,35.38&amp;year=-835">What was here in 835 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
